<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Amanishakheto</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. late 1st century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Amanishakheto was a ruling queen, or kandake, of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kushite-empire">Kingdom of Kush</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meroe">Meroë</a> in the late 1st century BCE. She is known from inscriptions and building work at several sites, and from her pyramid at Meroë, where in 1834 the Italian adventurer Giuseppe Ferlini found a treasure of gold jewellery now in the museums of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/amanishakheto.jpg" alt="Amanishakheto" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Khruner · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Relief_Amanishakheto_Munich.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
